1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,496
Every system starts with a problem.

2
00:00:03,796 --> 00:00:08,184
The difference lies in how we understand
it… and in what we can build from it.

3
00:00:12,384 --> 00:00:17,134
I'm João Pedro Soares de Figueiredo,
a full stack software engineer.

4
00:00:17,134 --> 00:00:22,059
I'm Brazilian, with a degree in Computer
Science from Centro Universitário UNIPÊ,

5
00:00:22,059 --> 00:00:27,046
and more than three years of experience in
software development, automation and IT

6
00:00:27,046 --> 00:00:28,032
support.

7
00:00:28,032 --> 00:00:32,322
Today I live in Granada, where I'm
studying for a Master's degree in Business

8
00:00:32,322 --> 00:00:36,344
Process Management and Technologies
at the University of Granada.

9
00:00:36,344 --> 00:00:41,217
That's the combination I care about:
software engineering and business processes,

10
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:42,840
working together.

11
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,518
The way I work starts from a simple idea:

12
00:00:45,518 --> 00:00:48,307
understand the problem before writing code.

13
00:00:48,307 --> 00:00:53,730
I look for the right solution, model the
data and components, implement, integrate

14
00:00:53,730 --> 00:00:56,246
the services, test and optimize.

15
00:00:56,246 --> 00:00:58,470
And at the end I ask one more question:

16
00:00:58,470 --> 00:01:01,694
what changes in the operation once this works?

17
00:01:02,294 --> 00:01:04,905
I've worked across different
layers of a product.

18
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:10,827
On the back end, with Java and Spring
Boot, Node.js, Python and PHP.

19
00:01:10,827 --> 00:01:15,367
On the front end, with React,
TypeScript and Next.js.

20
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:19,666
On the data side, with
SQL, PostgreSQL and MySQL.

21
00:01:19,666 --> 00:01:24,896
And in mobile and cross-platform apps
with Capacitor, connected to REST APIs,

22
00:01:24,896 --> 00:01:27,602
payments and external services.

23
00:01:27,702 --> 00:01:33,142
At Ouse Passar I was the main developer of
Código da Vida, an app for iOS, Android

24
00:01:33,142 --> 00:01:38,517
and the web: from data modeling to the
back end and the app store releases.

25
00:01:38,517 --> 00:01:41,236
I also worked on the Ouse Passar platform:

26
00:01:41,236 --> 00:01:46,350
payments and subscriptions with Hotmart
and RevenueCat, VPS infrastructure with

27
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:50,252
Nginx, load balancing and
SQL performance analysis.

28
00:01:50,652 --> 00:01:54,144
But technology matters when it
improves something concrete.

29
00:01:54,344 --> 00:02:00,058
At Inovatec-JP I contributed to E-PROCON,
the inspection system of PROCON in

30
00:02:00,058 --> 00:02:03,140
Paraíba, built with Java and Spring Boot:

31
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:08,577
digital field inspections, no pen
and paper, and automated reports.

32
00:02:08,577 --> 00:02:14,139
The result reported by the project: around
seventy percent less processing time.

33
00:02:15,139 --> 00:02:20,579
A team effort, with attention to interoperability
between public systems and to data

34
00:02:20,579 --> 00:02:23,186
protection under Brazil's LGPD.

35
00:02:23,186 --> 00:02:28,576
On SiCAPS, also for PROCON, I helped build
digital price collection and real-time

36
00:02:28,576 --> 00:02:30,573
geolocation tracking.

37
00:02:30,773 --> 00:02:35,902
At Sicredi, as a development intern, I
automated inactive-account management with

38
00:02:35,902 --> 00:02:41,061
UiPath and Python, freeing up about
ten percent of operational capacity.

39
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:44,993
And I modernized a legacy PHP PDF generator:

40
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:48,697
around five percent less processing time.

41
00:02:49,297 --> 00:02:53,194
Improving what already exists,
without rewriting everything:

42
00:02:53,194 --> 00:02:56,003
find the bottleneck and act on it.

43
00:02:56,603 --> 00:02:59,277
To me, artificial intelligence isn't magic:

44
00:02:59,277 --> 00:03:02,140
it's one more component of the system.

45
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:08,104
At Ouse Passar I built agents with OpenAI
and Gemini and automated workflows with

46
00:03:08,104 --> 00:03:12,949
n8n, connected to data, the
back end and external services.

47
00:03:12,949 --> 00:03:18,226
Beyond that, I've built AI agents that
automate complete workflows, assistants

48
00:03:18,226 --> 00:03:23,435
powered by large language models, and
image generation with Gemini, connected

49
00:03:23,435 --> 00:03:27,215
through n8n and APIs to
real data and products.

50
00:03:27,215 --> 00:03:30,783
And at Sicredi, automation
had a very concrete goal:

51
00:03:30,783 --> 00:03:35,225
remove repetitive work and
free up time for other tasks.

52
00:03:35,825 --> 00:03:38,392
I also like comparing alternatives with data.

53
00:03:38,392 --> 00:03:43,684
In my final degree project, together with
my classmates, we compared Spring Boot and

54
00:03:43,684 --> 00:03:45,470
Quarkus for Java APIs.

55
00:03:45,470 --> 00:03:51,725
We deployed equivalent APIs on Kubernetes
and load-tested them with JMeter.

56
00:03:51,725 --> 00:03:55,896
At idle, Spring Boot used
about 271 MiB of memory;

57
00:03:55,896 --> 00:03:57,997
Quarkus, about 21.

58
00:03:58,597 --> 00:04:03,337
But Spring Boot delivered lower latency and
higher throughput, while Quarkus handled

59
00:04:03,337 --> 00:04:07,907
the load with fewer replicas. The right
choice depends on the context and on what

60
00:04:07,907 --> 00:04:09,184
you measure.

61
00:04:09,384 --> 00:04:12,123
And I designed and built Shark Smokehouse:

62
00:04:12,123 --> 00:04:17,536
an e-commerce and management platform for
a shop and lounge in João Pessoa, already

63
